    <dashboard name='STORY ONTARIO RESEARCH FUNDING' type='storyboard'>
      <style>
        <style-rule element='story-point-caption'>
          <format attr='width' value='338' />
        </style-rule>
      </style>
      <size maxheight='964' maxwidth='1016' minheight='964' minwidth='1016' />
      <zones>
        <zone h='100000' id='2' type-v2='layout-basic' w='100000' x='0' y='0'>
          <zone h='98340' id='1' param='vert' removable='false' type-v2='layout-flow' w='98426' x='787' y='830'>
            <zone h='4357' id='3' type-v2='title' w='98426' x='787' y='830' />
            <zone fixed-size='425' h='44087' id='4' is-fixed='true' paired-zone-id='5' removable='false' type-v2='flipboard-nav' w='98426' x='787' y='5187' />
            <zone h='49896' id='5' paired-zone-id='4' removable='false' type-v2='flipboard' w='98426' x='787' y='49274'>
              <flipboard active-id='5' nav-type='caption' show-nav-arrows='true'>
                <story-points>
                  <story-point caption='Trends in Funding Allocation&#10;&#10;The fiscal years 2021–2022 and 2023–2024 saw the largest funding allocations, totaling over $200 million, indicating times of increased project investments or strategic initiatives.&#10;Significant Rise After 2020–21: There is a noticeable sharp increase in funding after 2020–21, most likely as a result of refocused policy efforts or pandemic-related research priorities.&#10;    Declining Trend in 2024–25: There is a notable drop in funding in 2024–25, which could be a sign of either a change in strategic focus or the completion of large projects.&#10;&#10;' captured-sheet='FUNDING ALLOCATION TRENDS' id='1' />
                  <story-point caption='Project Distribution By Research&#10;&#10;Lead in Medical and Health Sciences: With the largest funding (~119M), this field demonstrated its importance in Ontario&apos;s research agenda, which is probably influenced by healthcare priorities.&#10;Natural Sciences and Engineering Follow Closely: The importance of fields like engineering (99M) and natural sciences (104M) in promoting innovation and technology is highlighted.&#10;Neglected Areas: The low funding for disciplines like civil engineering (13.7M) and other research (17.8M) raises the possibility of underfunding critical infrastructure research. ' captured-sheet='PROJECT DISTRIBUTION BY RESEARCH' id='2' />
                  <story-point caption='Ontario&apos;s financial commitment to actual project costs&#10;&#10;The graph shows a significant gap &#10;between Ontario&apos;s commitments   &#10;(orange) and actual project costs (red)&#10; across fiscal years, with costs &#10;consistently exceeding commitments&#10;The gap widens significantly in 2021-22&#10; and peaks in 2023-24, indicating underfunding and rising project expenditures. Improved budget &#10;alignment and cost management are recommended.&#10;' captured-sheet='ONTARIO&apos;S COMMITMENT VS ACTUAL PROJECT COST' id='3' />
                  <story-point caption='Institute’s and Ontario Commitment&#10;&#10;The Line graph illustrates that Ontario&apos;s commitment (green) experienced a significant spike in 2021, followed by a decrease and a recovery in 2024. The Institute&apos;s commitment (red) showed a gradual and steady increase starting in 2022, while the other commitments (blue) remained flat with minimal change throughout.' captured-sheet='INSTITUTE&apos;S AND ONTARIO&apos;S COMMITMENT' id='4' />
                  <story-point caption='Top 5 Funding by City&#10;The map shows the funding amounts for different cities. Ottawa (shown in red) has the most funding, with 91,579,948. Hamilton (shown in orange) comes second with 69,529,990. The size of the markers on the map shows the amount of funding, and Ottawa stands out with the highest funding compared to other cities.' captured-sheet='TOP 5 FUNDING BY CITY' id='5'>
                    <capturedDeltas>
                      <worksheet name='TOP 5 FUNDING BY CITY'>
                        <style delta-type='added'>
                          <style-rule element='axis'>
                            <encoding attr='space' class='0' field='[federated.1haa01817kladr1er2kqi1exqpj4].[Latitude (generated)]' field-type='quantitative' max='6392925.5129356366' min='4180385.3984329738' projection='EPSG:3857' range-type='fixed' scope='rows' type='space' />
                            <encoding attr='space' class='0' field='[federated.1haa01817kladr1er2kqi1exqpj4].[Longitude (generated)]' field-type='quantitative' max='-7963746.0235729739' min='-11888143.487964598' projection='EPSG:3857' range-type='fixed' scope='cols' type='space' />
                          </style-rule>
                        </style>
                      </worksheet>
                    </capturedDeltas>
                  </story-point>
                </story-points>
              </flipboard>
            </zone>
          </zone>
          <zone-style>
            <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
            <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
            <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
            <format attr='margin' value='8' />
          </zone-style>
        </zone>
      </zones>
      <simple-id uuid='{C5D80AF7-EBF5-43E1-B927-134936C18E86}' />
